[Event "01. Key squares: pawn on the 4th rank (1/3)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/8/2KP4/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "01. Key squares: pawn on the 4th rank (1/3)"]
[LineDescription "Key squares of a pawn on the 2nd-4th rank: the three squares two ranks ahead of it."]

{[%csl Gc6,Gd6,Ge6] Welcome! This course masters ONE idea: king and pawn against
king. Rule 1: every pawn has KEY SQUARES. If your king reaches one of them, the
pawn will queen whatever Black does. For a pawn on the 4th rank they are the
three squares two ranks in front of it. Every line is played to the end, so you
will see the pawn become a queen. Pawn on d4: the key squares (green) are c6,
d6, e6.} 1. Kc5 {Diagonal opposition: next move it turns into a key square or
the direct opposition.} 1... Ke6 {[%csl Gc6,Gd6,Ge6]} 2. Kc6 {Key square c6!
With your king here the pawn will queen, whoever is to move. Now escort it all
the way.} 2... Ke7 {[%csl Gc6,Gd6,Ge6]} 3. d5 {Now the pawn advances, with the
king clearing the way.} 3... Kd8 {[%csl Gc6,Gd6,Ge6,Gc7,Gd7,Ge7]} 4. Kd6
{Opposition! The kings face each other with one square between them, and Black
must give way.} 4... Kc8 {[%csl Gc6,Gd6,Ge6,Gc7,Gd7,Ge7]} 5. Ke7 {The king walks
ahead of the pawn and clears the way; the pawn follows.} 5... Kc7 {[%csl
Gc6,Gd6,Ge6,Gc7,Gd7,Ge7]} 6. d6+ {Pawn forward, with check.} 6... Kc6 {[%csl
Gc7,Gd7,Ge7,Gc8,Gd8,Ge8]} 7. d7 {The pawn reaches the 7th rank and your king
guards the queening square: nothing can stop it now.} 7... Kc7 {[%csl Gd8]} 8.
d8=Q+ {Queen! The pawn has promoted safely, and king and queen against king is
an easy win.} *

[Event "01. Key squares: pawn on the 4th rank (2/3)"]
