[LineName "01. Key squares: pawn on the 4th rank (2/3)"]
[LineDescription "Key squares of a pawn on the 2nd-4th rank: the three squares two ranks ahead of it."]

{[%csl Gd6,Ge6,Gf6] Same rule: walk your king to a key square. Black may stand
in the way, so go around him. Pawn on e4: the key squares (green) are d6, e6,
f6.} 1. Kf5 {Diagonal opposition: next move it turns into a key square or the
direct opposition.} 1... Kd6 {[%csl Gd6,Ge6,Gf6]} 2. Kf6 {Key square f6! With
your king here the pawn will queen, whoever is to move. Now escort it all the
way.} 2... Kd7 {[%csl Gd6,Ge6,Gf6]} 3. e5 {Now the pawn advances, with the king
clearing the way.} 3... Ke8 {[%csl Gd6,Ge6,Gf6,Gd7,Ge7,Gf7]} 4. Ke6 {Opposition!
The kings face each other with one square between them, and Black must give
way.} 4... Kd8 {[%csl Gd6,Ge6,Gf6,Gd7,Ge7,Gf7]} 5. Kf7 {The king walks ahead of
the pawn and clears the way; the pawn follows.} 5... Kd7 {[%csl
Gd6,Ge6,Gf6,Gd7,Ge7,Gf7]} 6. e6+ {Pawn forward, with check.} 6... Kd6 {[%csl
Gd7,Ge7,Gf7,Gd8,Ge8,Gf8]} 7. e7 {The pawn reaches the 7th rank and your king
guards the queening square: nothing can stop it now.} 7... Kd7 {[%csl Ge8]} 8.
e8=Q+ {Queen! The pawn has promoted safely, and king and queen against king is
an easy win.} *

